.TH DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER 3 "2026-08-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER \- declare buffer for ranged memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER(
    name,
    smallest_size_power_of_two,
    largest_size_power_of_two,
    number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a uint8_t array sized correctly for a ranged memory pool with
the specified parameters. The buffer can then be passed to
r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). Automatically includes
header overhead for every sub-pool in the size calculation; the buffer
contains all sub-pools in contiguous memory.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name                                     Variable name for the buffer
smallest_size_power_of_two               Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two                Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Buffer is allocated on stack or in static storage depending on context
Useful for embedded systems or avoiding heap allocation
Buffer is declared with the alignment the pool's internal per-element
  header (a size_t plus a pointer) requires, so it can be handed
  directly to r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer() with no
  further alignment considerations; every individual sub-pool segment
  within the buffer stays correctly aligned as a consequence
Size is computed via CALCULATE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER_SIZE
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER(my_buffer, 4, 8, 10);
r_mempool *rmp = r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    my_buffer, sizeof(my_buffer), 4, 8, 10,
    fallback_disabled, false, NULL, NULL);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer (3)
